<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Abu'l-Fazl ibn Mubarak</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1551–1602</span> · Also known as Abul Fazl</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Abu'l-Fazl ibn Mubarak was the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vizier">vizier</a>, historian and close adviser of the Mughal emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/akbar">Akbar</a>. He wrote the Akbarnama, a history of Akbar's reign, with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ain-i-akbari">Ain-i-Akbari</a>, a detailed account of the empire's administration, and championed Akbar's policy of universal tolerance. He was killed in 1602 by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vir-singh-deo">Bir Singh Deo of Orchha</a> at the instigation of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jahangir">Prince Salim</a>, the future Jahangir.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/abul-fazl-ibn-mubarak.jpg" alt="Abu'l-Fazl ibn Mubarak" width="320" height="429" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Govardhan · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Abul_Fazl_cropped.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
